# LodgeX CH-11 Service Rating engineering rules

1. Read the package `README.md`, scoring manual, interaction manual, permission matrix, UI specifications and test plan before editing Service Rating code.
2. Inspect the repository and reuse existing LodgeX identity, company, project, schedule, Journey Management, LMS, arrival, workflow, notification, audit, event, file and authorization services.
3. Do not create duplicate systems of record.
4. The official grade is deterministic. AI may explain or forecast but may not calculate, assign, override, suppress or publish the official grade.
5. The overall grade is the worst applicable criterion. Never average grades.
6. A requires every applicable criterion to be A.
7. N/A is excluded and must not be confused with missing or stale data.
8. Approved effective-dated exceptions are applied before calculation.
9. Valid critical integrity/safety conditions force D.
10. B is yellow, C is orange, A is green and D is red in every UI.
11. In Crew Hub All Projects view, the overall grade is the lowest active project grade and the A/B/C/D distribution is shown separately.
12. Users do not routinely choose a grade from a dropdown. They correct facts, submit evidence, request review, approve an exception or apply a restricted critical override. The engine recalculates.
13. Snapshots and criterion results are immutable. Corrections create a new snapshot linked to the superseded result.
14. Active policy versions are immutable. Changes create a new draft/effective version.
15. Do not penalize a company solely because a required source is stale, conflicting or unavailable. Retain the last valid published grade and show Pending Data/Data Stale according to policy.
16. Enforce tenant, company, project, role, functional authority, approval authority and evidence-classification scope server-side. Frontend hiding is not authorization.
17. Do not perform direct cross-module table writes. Call authenticated application/domain services.
18. Events and recalculations must be idempotent and auditable. Publish events after the database transaction commits.
19. MCP and AI tools must inherit user permissions and call application services. Never query production MySQL directly.
20. Do not implement `set_grade`, `change_grade`, `hide_rating` or equivalent tools/endpoints.
21. Follow current repository ID, naming, module, TypeScript, testing, route and UI conventions. The package reference structure is guidance, not permission to create parallel architecture.
22. Add unit, feature, authorization, event/idempotency and UI tests for every material change.
23. Preserve loading, empty, N/A, Under Review, Data Stale, conflicting evidence, error and permission-denied states.
24. Do not claim production readiness while open decisions in `docs/14_Open_Decisions_and_Production_Gates.md` remain unresolved.
